class Solution {
  public int sortingPressFirstQualifyingSlip(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {class Sorter {int[]sort(int[]xs){if(xs.length<2)return xs;int m=xs.length/2;int[]left=sort(Arrays.copyOfRange(xs,0,m)),right=sort(Arrays.copyOfRange(xs,m,xs.length)),merged=new int[xs.length];int i=0,j=0,t=0;while(i<left.length||j<right.length)merged[t++]=j==right.length||(i<left.length&&left[i]<=right[j])?left[i++]:right[j++];return merged;}}for(int x:new Sorter().sort(a.clone()))out.add(x);}
    for(int x:out)if(x>=k)return x;return -1;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

Produce readings sorted numerically ascending using a merge-based divide-and-conquer approach. Keep duplicate readings.

Return the first value in the derived sequence that is at least k, or -1 if there is none. Return the value, not its index.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(n log n); space O(n).

